{
  "raw": {
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232306506": {
      "title": "Director of Student Accounts (Bursar)",
      "description": "Director of Student Accounts (Bursar). Essential Duties & Responsibilities Provide strategic and operational leadership for the University’s student accounts, billing, accounts receivable, cashiering, payment processing, refunds, collections, and reconciliation functions. Ensure accurate and timely assessment, processing, posting, reconciliation, and reporting of student financial transactions, including reconciliation of student accounts to the general ledger. Develop and execute the University’s accounts receivable and collections strategy, including monitoring aging, delinquency, collection performance, and overall financial exposure. Oversee payment arrangements, account holds, write-offs, promissory notes, third-party billing, and external collection-agency relationships in accordance with University policy. Establish and maintain effective internal financial controls, including segregation of duties, approval authorities, reconciliations, documentation, cash controls, and appropriate audit trails. Ensure compliance with applicable federal and state requirements, Department of Education regulations, FERPA, PCI-DSS, University policies, and other requirements governing student financial operations. Partner with Finance and internal/external auditors on financial reviews, audit activities, year-end processes, corrective actions, and resolution of identified control or compliance issues. Identify, assess, and mitigate financial, operational, compliance, and control risks associated with Bursar operations. Lead, supervise, develop, and evaluate Bursar/Student Accounts staff; establish clear performance expectations, accountability, service standards, cross-training, and appropriate backup coverage. Serve as the functional leader for Bursar-related student information, accounts receivable, payment, and financial systems, partnering with Finance and IT on implementations, integrations, testing, upgrades, and optimization. Establish and monitor key performance indicators for receivables, aging, collections, billing and transaction accuracy, refunds, reconciliations, service performance, and other critical Bursar operations. Lead continuous improvement, automation, standardization, and process redesign initiatives to strengthen financial controls, operational efficiency, service delivery, and business continuity. Provide leadership and resolution for complex or escalated student financial matters, ensuring policies are applied consistently and students and families receive accurate, timely, and professional service. Partner with Financial Aid, Registrar, Enrollment Management, Finance, Academic Affairs, IT, and other University stakeholders to address systemic issues and ensure effective coordination across the student financial lifecycle. Provide University leadership with financial analysis, operational insights, risk assessments, and recommendations related to student receivables, financial operations, policy, systems, and opportunities for improvement. Education Bachelor’s degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or a closely related field. Experience Minimum seven years of progressively responsible higher-education student financial services experience, including significant responsibility for student accounts, accounts receivable, billing, collections, refunds, and reconciliations. Minimum three years of demonstrated leadership experience in a Bursar, Deputy Bursar, Assistant Bursar, or comparable position with broad responsibility for student accounts, billing, receivables, collections, refunds, reconciliations, financial controls, compliance, and staff leadership. Minimum three years of direct supervisory/management experience, including responsibility for staff performance, development, and operational outcomes. Demonstrated experience managing the full student accounts lifecycle, rather than a single specialized component of Bursar operations. Demonstrated experience with financial controls, audit readiness, regulatory compliance, and operational risk management. Demonstrated experience analyzing accounts receivable aging and collection performance and implementing improvement strategies. Experience with student information systems, ERP/financial systems, electronic payment platforms, and related financial technology. Demonstrated experience leading significant process improvement, systems optimization, or operational change. Comparable titles may be considered when the candidate demonstrates equivalent breadth of responsibility, leadership accountability, decision authority, and Bursar-level operational experience."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232176700": {
      "title": "Sr. IT Support Lead",
      "description": "Sr. IT Support Lead. The Senior IT Support Lead serves as the senior technical resource for the IT Support team, delivering advanced technical support and operational leadership to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this position provides advanced end-user support, service desk operations, classroom and audiovisual technology support, endpoint administration, and IT asset management while serving as the primary escalation point for complex technical issues and operational support activities. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex incidents and service requests. Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex incidents and service requests, providing advanced troubleshooting and quality assurance before escalating to other Information Technology teams. Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Independent Judgment and Decision-Making Exercise independent judgment and decision-making by carrying out process frameworks and adapting to real world scenarios. Lead or coordinate technology deployments, operating system upgrades, software rollouts, hardware refresh initiatives, and other campus technology projects. Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex issues, and own escalations involving significant operational impacts. Mentor IT Support Technicians and student employees by providing technical guidance, knowledge sharing, and assistance with complex technical issues. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Serve as a secondary administrator for enterprise endpoint management platforms (e.g., Microsoft Intune, Ivanti, JAMF) by assisting with device administration, software deployments, policy testing, and endpoint management activities. Coordinate with vendors regarding equipment repairs, warranty claims, maintenance, break/fix services, product evaluations, and technical support requests. Test, evaluate, and validate new hardware, software, operating system updates, endpoint configurations, and other technologies before production deployment. Develop, review, and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, standard operating procedures, and technical standards. Participate in lifecycle planning, deployment planning, technology evaluation, and other operational planning meetings while providing technical recommendations. Assist with hardware and software asset management activities, including inventory reconciliation, lifecycle tracking, procurement coordination, and technology deployment documentation. Participate in technology projects, continuous service improvement initiatives, and departmental professional development activities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster’s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Requisite Experience* Bachelor's degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, Information Systems, or a related field preferred. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively responsible experience providing enterprise desktop support, service desk operations, endpoint management, classroom technology support, or related technical support services. Demonstrated experience serving as a technical lead, senior technician, or primary escalation point for complex technical issues. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused technology environment preferred. Experience providing advanced troubleshooting for Windows, macOS, Microsoft 365, endpoint management platforms, networking and cybersecurity basic fundamentals, audiovisual technologies, and enterprise business applications. Experience coordinating technology deployments, hardware refresh initiatives, software rollouts, operating system upgrades, or other campus technology projects. Experience working with endpoint management platforms such as Microsoft Intune, Ivanti, JAMF, or similar enterprise device management solutions. Experience coordinating with technology vendors regarding equipment repairs, warranty services, product evaluations, maintenance, and break/fix support. Experience developing and maintaining technical documentation, knowledge base articles, standard operating procedures, and technical standards. Experience supporting IT asset management activities, including hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, asset reconciliation, procurement coordination, and technology deployments. Experience testing, validating, and evaluating new hardware, software, operating system updates, endpoint policies, and enterprise technology solutions before production deployment."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232176300": {
      "title": "IT Support Technician",
      "description": "IT Support Technician. The IT Support Technician delivers comprehensive technical support and operational services to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this role is responsible for providing end-user support, managing service desk operations, supporting classroom and audiovisual technologies, and assisting with IT asset management. The position plays a key role in ensuring the University’s technology environment remains reliable, secure, and efficient. The IT Support Technician installs, configures, maintains, troubleshoots, and supports University-owned hardware, software, classroom technology, multimedia systems, and other IT-supported equipment while providing exceptional customer service through multiple service channels. This position also collaborates with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support hardware and software deployments, asset lifecycle management, classroom instruction, IT-supported events, documentation, knowledge management, operational projects, and strategic technology initiatives across Webster University locations. Position-specific goals and objectives are established annually and align with the mission, vision, and strategic goals of Information Technology and Webster University. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Assist with hardware and software deployments, technology refresh initiatives, lifecycle replacement, asset audits, inventory reconciliation, and equipment retirement following policies and procedures. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Participate in technology projects, campus deployments, operational initiatives, and continuous service improvement efforts. Collaborate with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support daily operations and strategic technology initiatives. Develop and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, procedures, and operational resources. Participate in professional development activities such as remaining current with emerging technologies, industry best practices, and departmental training opportunities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster’s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Technical Skills* Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, and mobile device operating systems. Knowledge of desktop, laptop, printer, peripheral, and audiovisual hardware installation, configuration, troubleshooting, and maintenance. Experience supporting classroom technology, conference room technology, multimedia systems, and collaboration platforms such as Microsoft Teams. Experience with endpoint management and device administration tools such as Microsoft Intune, JAMF, or similar platforms. Experience using IT service management and asset management systems to manage incidents, service requests, inventory, and technology assets. Knowledge of Microsoft 365 services, identity and access management, two-factor authentication, and basic account administration. Working knowledge of networking concepts, including TCP/IP, DNS, DHCP, wireless networking, VPN connectivity, and remote support technologies. Demonstrated troubleshooting and problem-solving skills across hardware, software, network, audiovisual, and endpoint technologies. Familiarity with software deployment, hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, and technology refresh processes. Understanding of cybersecurity best practices, endpoint security, phishing awareness, and data protection principles. Requisite Experience* Associate degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant work experience. Experience providing technical support in an end-user computing, service desk, desktop support, or information technology environment. Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, mobile devices, and common business applications. Experience using an IT service management system to manage incidents, service requests, and technology assets. Experience supporting classroom technology, audiovisual systems, or event technology is preferred. Experience with IT asset management, inventory control, hardware deployments, or lifecycle management is preferred. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused environment is preferred."
    },
