hones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex incidents and service requests, providing advanced troubleshooting and quality assurance before escalating to other Information Technology teams. Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Independent Judgment and Decision-Making Exercise independent judgment and decision-making by carrying out process frameworks and adapting to real world scenarios. Lead or coordinate technology deployments, operating system upgrades, software rollouts, hardware refresh initiatives, and other campus technology projects. Serve as the primary technical escalation point for complex issues, and own escalations involving significant operational impacts. Mentor IT Support Technicians and student employees by providing technical guidance, knowledge sharing, and assistance with complex technical issues. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Serve as a secondary administrator for enterprise endpoint management platforms (e.g., Microsoft Intune, Ivanti, JAMF) by assisting with device administration, software deployments, policy testing, and endpoint management activities. Coordinate with vendors regarding equipment repairs, warranty claims, maintenance, break/fix services, product evaluations, and technical support requests. Test, evaluate, and validate new hardware, software, operating system updates, endpoint configurations, and other technologies before production deployment. Develop, review, and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, standard operating procedures, and technical standards. Participate in lifecycle planning, deployment planning, technology evaluation, and other operational planning meetings while providing technical recommendations. Assist with hardware and software asset management activities, including inventory reconciliation, lifecycle tracking, procurement coordination, and technology deployment documentation. Participate in technology projects, continuous service improvement initiatives, and departmental professional development activities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster’s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Requisite Experience* Bachelor's degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, Information Systems, or a related field preferred. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively responsible experience providing enterprise desktop support, service desk operations, endpoint management, classroom technology support, or related technical support services. Demonstrated experience serving as a technical lead, senior technician, or primary escalation point for complex technical issues. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused technology environment preferred. Experience providing advanced troubleshooting for Windows, macOS, Microsoft 365, endpoint management platforms, networking and cybersecurity basic fundamentals, audiovisual technologies, and enterprise business applications. Experience coordinating technology deployments, hardware refresh initiatives, software rollouts, operating system upgrades, or other campus technology projects. Experience working with endpoint management platforms such as Microsoft Intune, Ivanti, JAMF, or similar enterprise device management solutions. Experience coordinating with technology vendors regarding equipment repairs, warranty services, product evaluations, maintenance, and break/fix support. Experience developing and maintaining technical documentation, knowledge base articles, standard operating procedures, and technical standards. Experience supporting IT asset management activities, including hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, asset reconciliation, procurement coordination, and technology deployments. Experience testing, validating, and evaluating new hardware, software, operating system updates, endpoint policies, and enterprise technology solutions before production deployment."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001232173706": {
      "title": "IT Support Technician",
      "description": "IT Support Technician. The IT Support Technician delivers comprehensive technical support and operational services to faculty, staff, students, and authorized affiliates across Webster University. As a member of the Infrastructure & Operations team, this role is responsible for providing end-user support, managing service desk operations, supporting classroom and audiovisual technologies, and assisting with IT asset management. The position plays a key role in ensuring the University’s technology environment remains reliable, secure, and efficient. The IT Support Technician installs, configures, maintains, troubleshoots, and supports University-owned hardware, software, classroom technology, multimedia systems, and other IT-supported equipment while providing exceptional customer service through multiple service channels. This position also collaborates with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support hardware and software deployments, asset lifecycle management, classroom instruction, IT-supported events, documentation, knowledge management, operational projects, and strategic technology initiatives across Webster University locations. Position-specific goals and objectives are established annually and align with the mission, vision, and strategic goals of Information Technology and Webster University. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* End User Support & Service Desk Respond to incidents, service requests, and technical support inquiries in accordance with established service level agreements (SLAs). Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support University-owned hardware, software, operating systems, mobile devices, printers, peripherals, and related technologies. Provide technical support through in-person, remote, telephone, email, and virtual support channels. This includes providing strong follow-up and claim ownership. Document, update, and maintain accurate records of incidents, service requests, assets, and work performed within the university's IT service management (ITSM) system. Diagnose and resolve hardware, software, network connectivity, account access, and other end-user technology issues, escalating requests when appropriate. Perform routine system maintenance, updates, equipment checks, and technology evaluations to ensure reliable service delivery. Classroom, AV, and Event Technology Support Provide technical support for classrooms, laboratories, conference rooms, and IT-supported events across Webster University locations. Install, configure, maintain, troubleshoot, and support audiovisual equipment including projectors, displays, microphones, speakers, cameras, control systems, and collaboration technologies. Support classroom instruction, webinars, hybrid meetings, livestreams, and other multimedia initiatives. Perform routine classroom technology inspections and coordinate equipment repairs, maintenance, and vendor service as needed. Assist with multimedia documentation, user training, and technology best practices. IT Asset Management & Technology Maintain accurate inventory records for university-owned technology assets using approved asset management and IT service management systems. Assist with hardware and software deployments, technology refresh initiatives, lifecycle replacement, asset audits, inventory reconciliation, and equipment retirement following policies and procedures. Support software implementation, asset tracking, procurement coordination, receiving, staging, tagging, deployment, surplus processing, and inventory reporting. Ensure asset records, inventory documentation, and related systems remain accurate, complete, and current. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Participate in technology projects, campus deployments, operational initiatives, and continuous service improvement efforts. Collaborate with Information Technology teams, campus departments, vendors, and student employees to support daily operations and strategic technology initiatives. Develop and maintain technical documentation, knowledge base articles, procedures, and operational resources. Participate in professional development activities such as remaining current with emerging technologies, industry best practices, and departmental training opportunities. Perform other duties as assigned. This position is generally scheduled to work Monday through Friday between 7:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Regular, in-person attendance on Webster’s campus is an essential function and physical requirement of the job. Work schedules are based on departmental and operational needs and may vary to support University operations. Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support classroom technology, IT-supported events, system implementations, maintenance activities, emergency response, and other operational requirements. This position is eligible for the University's Onsite Flex Work program in accordance with university policy and departmental operational requirements. Participation is based on job responsibilities, operational needs, and supervisory approval. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed. Functional Skills Can perform excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including but not limited to; Teams, phone, email, in person, and the ticket management system. Demonstrates strong ticket management and communication skills by thoroughly documenting troubleshooting steps, actions taken, findings, and resolutions to ensure accurate case tracking, knowledge sharing, and seamless issue handoff when needed. Demonstrated commitment to providing exceptional customer service with professionalism, patience, and a customer-focused approach. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet established deadlines. Ability to analyze problems, exercise sound judgment, and independently resolve technical issues while recognizing when escalation is appropriate. Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment while also working independently with minimal supervision. Ability to adapt to changing technologies, priorities, and operational needs in a fast-paced environment. Ability to develop and maintain technical documentation, procedures, and knowledge resources. Ability to maintain confidentiality and safeguard sensitive information while complying with university policies and applicable regulations (e.g., FERPA, HIPAA, Title IX, and security awareness requirements). Technical Skills* Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, and mobile device operating systems. Knowledge of desktop, laptop, printer, peripheral, and audiovisual hardware installation, configuration, troubleshooting, and maintenance. Experience supporting classroom technology, conference room technology, multimedia systems, and collaboration platforms such as Microsoft Teams. Experience with endpoint management and device administration tools such as Microsoft Intune, JAMF, or similar platforms. Experience using IT service management and asset management systems to manage incidents, service requests, inventory, and technology assets. Knowledge of Microsoft 365 services, identity and access management, two-factor authentication, and basic account administration. Working knowledge of networking concepts, including TCP/IP, DNS, DHCP, wireless networking, VPN connectivity, and remote support technologies. Demonstrated troubleshooting and problem-solving skills across hardware, software, network, audiovisual, and endpoint technologies. Familiarity with software deployment, hardware lifecycle management, inventory control, and technology refresh processes. Understanding of cybersecurity best practices, endpoint security, phishing awareness, and data protection principles. Requisite Experience* Associate degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant work experience. Experience providing technical support in an end-user computing, service desk, desktop support, or information technology environment. Experience supporting Windows, MacOS, mobile devices, and common business applications. Experience using an IT service management system to manage incidents, service requests, and technology assets. Experience supporting classroom technology, audiovisual systems, or event technology is preferred. Experience with IT asset management, inventory control, hardware deployments, or lifecycle management is preferred. Experience working in a higher education or similarly complex customer-focused environment is preferred."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001220008406": {
      "title": "Learning Systems Support Lead",
      "description": "Learning Systems Support Lead. The Learning Systems Support Lead provides day-to-day technical and functional support for the University’s learning management system (LMS) and related teaching and learning technologies. The Analyst supports faculty, students, and staff and serves as a primary point of contact for Instructional/Educational Technology-related support requests from the University IT Service Desk and other University support teams. Support requests are received through the University’s service management system, OLC Support email, telephone, and referrals from other University offices. The Analyst monitors and manages this incoming support activity; troubleshoots and resolves technical and administrative issues; and triages, escalates, coordinates, and follows up on issues requiring assistance from LMS Administration, Information Technology, Enrollment, Instructional Design, or other functional and technical areas. The Analyst also serves as the backup LMS Administrator. In addition to providing direct support, the Learning Systems Support Lead serves as the primary operational owner of learning systems support function, including processes, workflows, escalation pathways, documentation, and self-help resources. The Lead analyzes support activity to identify recurring issues and trends, investigates root causes, maintains support processes and self-help resources, and coordinates with appropriate University offices to address recurring issues and improve the effectiveness of the support model. The Analyst also assists with course maintenance and other learning systems responsibilities as needed. Reports to the Director, Online Learning Center. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* Provide day-to-day technical and functional support for the LMS and related teaching and learning technologies, including resolving technical and administrative issues for faculty, students, and staff. Provide Instructional/Educational Technology-related support to the University's Information Technology Service Desk and other University support teams, troubleshooting issues and coordinating escalation or resolution with appropriate functional and technical areas. Manage the day-to-day learning systems support operation, including incoming requests received through the University's service management system, OLC Support email, telephone, and other support channels; assess and prioritize requests and resolve, triage, escalate, coordinate, and follow up on issues through appropriate resolution. Participate in live support activities via video conferencing and chat as necessary and required. Analyze support activity and service data to identify recurring issues, trends, and opportunities for improvement; lead investigation of root causes and coordinate cross-functional resolution of systemic or recurring support issues with appropriate University offices. Develop, maintain, and improve support documentation, FAQs, self-help resources, standard procedures, and other knowledge resources for faculty, students, staff, and University support teams. Establish, maintain, and continuously improve learning systems support processes, workflows, service practices, communications, escalation pathways, and other resources to ensure effective and consistent support and reduce recurring support needs. Build and maintain course content in Canvas using the Canvas Rich Content Editor and HTML/CSS as appropriate. Support the Instructional Design team in the maintenance of online courses, including updating course content and resolving content-related technical issues. Collaborate with faculty to make minor course content updates, including correcting broken links, formatting pages, linking files, and addressing other course content issues. Serve as backup LMS Administrator and assist with LMS administration and learning systems operations as needed. Participate in after-hours support as required to maintain University responsiveness to user support issues. Experience using and navigating a learning management system (LMS), preferably Canvas. Experience using Microsoft 365 and common teaching and learning technologies. Demonstrated technical troubleshooting, analytical, and problem-solving skills, including the ability to investigate issues, identify potential root causes, and determine appropriate next steps. Ability to recognize patterns and recurring issues across support requests and identify opportunities for process, system, documentation, or support improvements. Excellent customer service and communication skills, including the ability to interpret and communicate technical information clearly and effectively to users with varying levels of technical expertise. Ability to develop clear, accurate, and user-friendly technical documentation, procedures, FAQs, and self-help resources. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail, with the ability to prioritize and manage multiple support requests, issues, and responsibilities. Ability to learn new systems and technologies effectively and independently. Demonstrated ability to work productively across functional and technical teams and coordinate the resolution of issues involving multiple stakeholders. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, and take ownership of issues through appropriate resolution, escalation, or coordination. Demonstrated ability to lead or coordinate a technical support function, workflow, or service, including establishing priorities, coordinating work across stakeholders, and improving support processes. 3-5 years of progressively responsible experience supporting a learning management system, enterprise application, instructional technology environment, or related technical support function. Experience coordinating technical support workflows, service operations, or cross-functional issue resolution preferred. Bachelor's degree in informational technology, instructional technology, education, business, or related field required. An equivalent combination of education and substantial progressively responsible relevant experience may be considered."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001231156000": {
      "title": "Electronic Resources Librarian",
      "description": "Electronic Resources Librarian. General Statement of Duties: The Electronic Resources Librarian is responsible for ensuring reliable access to the library's electronic collections and online services. The position manages the lifecycle of electronic resources, serves as the primary contact for access and troubleshooting issues, and works collaboratively with vendors, library staff, consortia partners, and university IT to resolve resource access problems. The librarian plays a key role in maintaining and improving the systems that support resource discovery and delivery, including the integrated library system, authentication services, discovery tools, and related technologies. This position contributes to the implementation and optimization of library systems and helps develop efficient, user-centered workflows that improve access to information resources. Responsibilities draw heavily from the library's current electronic resources management functions while maintaining a meaningful role in systems administration and migration activities. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* Electronic Resources Access, Troubleshooting, and Management (55%) Manages the lifecycle of electronic resources, including evaluation, ordering, implementation, activation, maintenance, renewal support, payments, and cancellation. Serves as the library's primary contact for electronic resource access issues and troubleshooting. Monitors and responds to reports of problems affecting databases, eBooks, e-journals, streaming media, and other online resources. Investigates and resolves access problems in collaboration with vendors, University IT, and other partners. Maintains high service standards for responding to electronic resource issues and communicating status updates to stakeholders. Maintains documentation related to electronic resources, licensing terms, administrative credentials, invoicing, and workflows. Tracks vendor communications regarding content changes, platform changes, outages, and service disruptions. Monitors availability and performance of electronic resources; proactively identifies and resolves potential issues. Supports management of the library's materials and membership budget lines. Each year analyzes spending patterns and as a member of the Library Budget Task Force, participates in developing a financial forecast to guide the library's budget request. Reviews financial records for selected library materials appropriations and expenditures. Reconciles account balances between the library's records and Webster's accounting system. Regularly communicates with library's Management Team and subject librarians regarding budget position; ensures that funds are expended appropriately and in a timely manner. Works closely with the finance department to ensure that the library's financial processes adhere to Webster and accounting best practices. Leads implementation of financial process changes in the library. Reviews invoices and submits purchase requisitions and invoices through Webster's accounting systems. Maintains accurate electronic resource records within Library systems. Library System Administration and Technology (25%) Serves as a library systems administrator for the integrated library system and related applications. Serves as a co-lead on system implementation, migration, testing, configuration, and ongoing maintenance activities. Maintains authentication services, proxy configuration, discovery services, and other tools supporting access to library resources. Collaborates with library staff to improve workflows through effective use of library technologies. Maintains documentation for system configuration and procedures. Evaluates emerging technologies and recommends improvements that enhance access and operational efficiency. Assessment, Reporting, and Data Management (10%) Collects, analyzes, and reports usage statistics for electronic resources. Develops reports and dashboards that support collection and operational decision making. Coordinates collection assessment and resource review projects. Maintains the databases that feed the library’s Publication Finder and link resolver; adds, modifies, or deletes resources from these databases as appropriate. Participates in data cleanup, migration, and quality-control initiatives. Provides data and analysis to support strategic planning and collection management decisions. Liaison and Professional Responsibilities (10%) Serves as liaison to one or more academic departments. Participates in collection development activities for assigned subject areas. Provides research consultations and instructional support as appropriate. Serves on library, university, consortium, and professional committees. Engages in professional development and remains current with developments in electronic resources, library systems, and academic librarianship. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities Serves on Library and University committees as appropriate Works at service desk as staff back-up when needed May supervise student employees and coordinate staff assigned to projects related to electronic resources, systems and data management. Perform other duties as assigned. Master's degree in Library and Information Science from an ALA-accredited institution or equivalent. Experience managing or supporting electronic resources in a library environment. Experience troubleshooting electronic resource access issues. Strong customer service orientation and commitment to responsive problem solving. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively with vendors, colleagues, and end users. Strong organizational, analytical, and project management skills. Ability to prioritize multiple responsibilities and adapt to changing needs. Ability to work independently and collaboratively. Minimum of three-years of library experience or comparable experience"
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001227908806": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor—Stage Management Instructor/Mentor",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor—Stage Management Instructor/Mentor. Webster University’s Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts invites applications for the position of Stage Management Instructor/Mentor for Stage Management majors. Essential Duties Teach Stage Management 1 Teach Stage Management Topics: Non-Traditional Work Teach 4 th year Professional Practice Teach Stage Management Seminar Mentor student stage managers on four Conservatory productions and one production for the Dance program Host prospective students in classes and on campus as able; join prospective interviews with other production faculty as schedule permits Bachelor's degree and/or extensive experience in the field"
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001200918506": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Admissions Counselor",
      "description": "Undergraduate Admissions Counselor. The Undergraduate Admissions Counselor identifies, recruits, and contributes to increasing the population of admitted and enrolled undergraduate students (freshmen and transfer) and is responsible for program development and implementation. The Counselor must be familiar with successful recruitment practices and must have a naturally enthusiastic and persuasive manner to recruit and convert undergraduate students to enroll at Webster University. Additional responsibility for this position will include admissions-related file processing and supporting the admissions operations to increase undergraduate students at Webster USA campuses. This position reports to the Associate Director, Admissions. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* ​ ​Recruitment/Territory Management – 35% ​Recruits new freshmen and/or transfer students to Webster University and assists with recruiting plans and events both on and off campus. ​Manages an assigned geographic territory and travels to high schools, community colleges, community-based organizations; attend college fairs and conduct special programs across the country. ​Plan all aspects of travel, manage receipts, and prepare travel reports to meet policies and deadlines. ​Represents undergraduate admissions in a public-speaking capacity both on and off campus and maintains records of these activities in SLATE. Admissions/Advising – 50% ​Responsible for counseling and outreach to students and parents on the admissions requirements and procedures, and works directly with them to resolve extenuating circumstances, complaints and appeals ​Manages data, prospects, applicants, and recruitment territories through the SLATE (CRM) system to enhance student relationships, communication timelines and to connect students with other areas of the university. ​Cultivate student applicant pool and assist with the transition to campus for incoming students ​In accordance with university guidelines and enrollment objectives, determines eligibility of applicants for admissions based on analysis of the admission application, transcripts, standardized tests, and supplemental documentation. ​Utilizes professional judgement to analyze the complete student file to determine full admission, admission by exception, denial or to send the application on for further review. ​Responds, within established time benchmarks and service delivery protocols, to inquiries regarding admissions status from prospective students. ​Provides assistance to prospective students regarding admissions requirements and process. ​Updates information in SLATE and other university systems. ​Maintains a high level of working knowledge of university information, organization, opportunities, and policies ​Responsible for advising and counseling special populations that may include but are not limited to athletes, rural students, urban students, conservatory, honors, competitive scholars, etc. ​Programming – 10% ​Assists and participates in a variety of on-campus and off-campus recruitment and yield programs, virtually and in person ​Implements recruitment activities such as application workshops, information sessions, financial aid information sessions and outreach presentations. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* ​ ​Other duties and Assignments – 5% ​Cultivates working relationships with university schools and colleges, departments, counselors and advisors to enhance student service and satisfaction as well as establish community contacts ​Reports statistical data on programs, projects, SLATE cases and travel activities ​Serves on internal committees and task forces and participates in work groups to support the mission of Enrollment Management and Webster University QUALIFICATIONS FOR APPOINTMENT Functional/Technical Skills* ​ ​Skill in operating a computer and supporting software ​​Knowledge of immigration rules and regulations. ​Knowledge of college admissions policies and procedures. ​Skill in reviewing/evaluating transcripts. ​Strong interpersonal communication skills. ​Ability to demonstrate cultural sensitivity and diplomacy. ​Ability to effectively communicate in oral and written form. ​Ability to learn and apply applicable federal regulations. ​Ability to work independently without direct supervision. ​A working knowledge of higher education within the United States. Requisite Experience* One year of experience in recruitment, admissions, advising, or related enrollment and/or student service experience. Preferred Experience in higher education This position is eligible to advance to Sr. Undergraduate Admissions Counselor position after 2 years at counselor level and based on meeting performance expectations. Standard Schedule: ​Days 8:30am – 4:30pm with occasional evening and weekend hours. ​ If appropriate indicate if work is fully on-site. If under a Flexible/Remote Work Arrangement, add “This position is approved for a <enter specific arrangement: compressed work week, on-site flexible, 1- or 2- day hybrid, or fully remote> work arrangement as offered in Webster University’s Flexible and/or Remote Work Arrangements policy. Use by individual employees is at the discretion of the supervisor and unit leadership based on department and University needs”. *Any one position may not include all of the duties listed, nor do the examples necessarily include all of the duties performed ​ Bachelor's Degree and one year of experience in recruitment, admissions, advising, or related enrollment and/or student service experience."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001227036006": {
      "title": "Director, Graduate Recruitment & Admissions",
      "description": "Director, Graduate Recruitment & Admissions. General Statement of Duties : The Director, Graduate Enrollment provides operational leadership for graduate recruitment, admissions decision-making, and enrollment across Webster University's St. Louis campus, San Antonio campus, and U.S. domestic online programs. Reporting to the Associate Vice President for Strategic Enrollment Management/SIO, the Director is responsible for executing enrollment strategies that achieve institutional enrollment and revenue goals while ensuring timely, consistent, and student-centered admissions decisions. This is a highly tactical, performance-oriented leadership position. The Director leads graduate admissions counselors, oversees admissions file review and decision processes, partners with the admissions processing team, manages enrollment performance in real time, and implements data-driven and AI-enabled solutions to improve engagement and conversion throughout the enrollment funnel. Enrollment Leadership - 20% Execute graduate enrollment strategies aligned with institutional goals and revenue targets. Develop annual, term, and weekly recruitment plans and lead forecasting and enrollment planning. Monitor performance and implement corrective strategies to achieve enrollment goals. Recruitment Operations - 15% Direct graduate recruitment activities for St. Louis, San Antonio, and online domestic programs. Set recruitment priorities, counselor assignments, event strategy, and outreach expectations. Oversee graduate recruitment events, virtual sessions, campus visits, and appropriate travel. Admissions Decision & processing - 20% Serve as a senior admissions decision-maker, reviewing completed graduate application files and rendering admissions decisions in accordance with University policy. Review complex, borderline, and exception cases; exercise professional judgment and ensure consistency in admissions decisions. Partner with the admissions processing team to establish file-review priorities, resolve documentation issues, and improve application-to-decision turnaround times. Enrollment Funnel, AI & Engagement - 20% Own performance across the graduate enrollment funnel and analyze conversion trends, stalled populations, and operational barriers. Identify, implement, and manage relevant AI-enabled recruitment and engagement solutions to support lead prioritization, personalized outreach, application completion, yield, and anti-melt efforts. Evaluate AI and automation effectiveness and integrate solutions appropriately with CRM workflows and counselor engagement strategies. Team Leadership - 10% Supervise, coach, and develop graduate admissions counselors and establish clear performance expectations and accountability. Conduct regular pipeline, productivity, and admissions quality reviews and intervene when performance falls below expectations. Data, CRM, Analytics & Collaboration - 15% Utilize CRM, enrollment technologies, dashboards, and forecasts to guide decisions and monitor counselor productivity and data quality. Partner with Enrollment Technology, academic leadership, marketing, financial aid, admissions processing, operations, and student success. Support new program launches, market expansion, and continuous improvement of graduate enrollment processes. Five or more years of enrollment management or admissions leadership experience. Bachelor’s Degree required. Preference: Graduate Enrollment/Recruitment/Admissions Functional/Technical Skills* Demonstrated success leading graduate recruitment and admissions teams. Experience reviewing graduate application files and making or overseeing admissions decisions. Ability to exercise professional judgment in complex, exception, and non-routine admissions cases. Strong leadership, analytical, communication, organizational, and time-management skills. Experience with CRM and enrollment technologies; Slate experience preferred. Ability to evaluate and implement AI, automation, and digital engagement solutions in an enrollment environment. Ability to use data and technology to improve operational efficiency, student engagement, and enrollment outcomes."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001215314106": {
      "title": "Community Relations Coordinator",
      "description": "Community Relations Coordinator. The Community Relations Coordinator promotes community awareness of the Community Music School of Webster University among current and prospective students, parents, educators, key influencers for potential students, and potential donors. Markets and recruits students for all CMS programs. Manages the CMS website, social media accounts, and print and digital marketing materials. Coordinates all marketing efforts in conjunction with key administrators and the Webster Global Marketing Communications and LGCFA office. Reports to the CMS Director. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Serves as marketing liaison among the Community Music School (CMS), Webster University, and the community at large. Raises visibility in the region for the CMS among potential donors and key influencers for potential students. Monitors and updates the CMS website and social media accounts. Develops and monitors marketing and advertising plan with CMS Director. Designs, produces, and distributes all CMS digital and print materials. Communicates with constituents via email using Emma email system. Creates and distributes monthly e- newsletters. Informs local media of CMS events, programs and student/faculty accomplishments. Produces publications of the Community Music School with CMS staff for copy, graphic designers, printers, and mailing sources. Coordinates photo sessions for publications and digital marketing. Represents the School at targeted conferences and events. Creates and updates displays. Collects, monitors, and evaluates data regarding past, current, and prospective students and parents to inform marketing practices. Monitors and updates mailing and email lists. Assists with planning and implementing special events. Develops plans to connect with alumni and promote alumni programs. Provides information about the CMS programs to libraries, schools, clubs, and relevant venues Monitors trends in music education and related activities. Prepares student enrollment reports, NASM enrollment report and organization data. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities Represents CMS at 2-3 summer recruitment fairs per year. Hosts some School Recitals and coordinates annual Faculty Showcase. Monday – Friday, 8:30 a.m. – 4:30 p.m. remote position; occasional on-site and weekend work required. Requisite Experience Minimum of 5 years in community relations, marketing, recruitment and/or project management. Functional/technical Skills Bachelor’s degree or equivalent experience required. Strong organizational skills and effective interpersonal skills. Able to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. Must be competent with Excel, Microsoft Word, Office 365, Teams, student management systems, websites, and social media. Experienced with Constant Contact, Emma, or similar email systems. Ability to exercise important human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the University and community."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001225820606": {
      "title": "Guest Director for Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts—Carrie: The Musical",
      "description": "Guest Director for Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts—Carrie: The Musical. Webster University’s Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts invites applications for the position of Guest D irector for Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts’ Spring P roduction of CARRIE: THE MUSICAL Directors set their artistic vision for a show , including selecting the student cast, collaborating with the team of student designers, leading rehearsals, blocking /choreographing the action, and guiding the overall aesthetic of the production . Guest directors will: - Attend semester auditions to select the student cast members - Lead rehearsals - Monitor production's pacing - Collaborate with production team, including student stage managers - Collaborate with team of student designers - Collaborate with producer and faculty to ensure artistic process aligns with educational goals of the department - Deliver a high-caliber production Bachelor's degree and/or extensive experience in the field"
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001216751206": {
      "title": "Sr. HR Partner: Employee Relations & Institutional Compliance",
      "description": "Sr. HR Partner: Employee Relations & Institutional Compliance. The Senior HR Partner, Employee Relations & Institutional Compliance serves as a senior individual contributor responsible for providing advanced consultation and independently managing complex employee relations, civil rights, workplace investigation, accommodation, and institutional compliance matters. The Senior HR Partner exercises significant independent judgment in managing assigned matters, advising leaders, conducting investigations, identifying organizational risk, and developing practical recommendations. Complex, high-risk, precedent-setting, or institutionally significant matters are escalated to the Director, who retains functional ownership, program oversight, and decision authority. Essential Duties and Responsibilities 1. Employee Relations & Leader Consultation – 30% Provide advanced consultation to leaders regarding employee relations, performance management, corrective action, workplace conflict, employee complaints, policy application, and other workplace matters. Independently assess complex employee concerns and recommend appropriate courses of action consistent with University policies and practices. Coach leaders on effective issue resolution, documentation, performance expectations, and employee communication. Support employee relations matters associated with organizational and workforce changes. Identify recurring trends, organizational risks, and opportunities for proactive intervention. 2. Civil Rights Compliance & Workplace Investigations – 25% Conduct prompt, thorough, impartial, and appropriately documented investigations involving discrimination, harassment, retaliation, sexual misconduct, workplace misconduct, and other policy concerns. Conduct investigative planning; complainant, respondent, and witness interviews; gathering testimonial, documentary and electronic evidence; trauma-informed investigative practices; and preparation of comprehensive investigative reports. Support matters arising under Title VI, Title VII, Title IX, and other applicable civil rights and employment requirements. Maintain appropriate neutrality, discretion, confidentiality, and professional judgment when handling sensitive matters. Escalate complex or significant institutional risks to the Director/Title IX Coordinator. 3. ADA & Workplace Accommodations – 20% Administer assigned workplace accommodation matters, including intake, documentation, interactive process discussions, case management, and follow-up. Consult with employees and leaders regarding essential job functions, workplace limitations, potential accommodations, and operational considerations. Develop accommodation recommendations consistent with established University procedures and applicable requirements. Maintain appropriate confidentiality of medical and accommodation information. Escalate complex, high-risk, disputed, or potentially precedent-setting accommodation matters to the Director. 4. Case Management, Compliance & Risk – 15% Manage assigned employee relations, civil rights, investigation, and accommodation cases from intake through resolution. Maintain accurate, timely, and confidential case documentation and monitor applicable deadlines and follow-up requirements. Apply established employment and institutional policies consistently and identify matters requiring policy interpretation or escalation. Analyze case trends and data to identify systemic concerns, emerging risks, and opportunities for process improvement. Support compliance assessments, reporting, audits, and other institutional risk-management activities as assigned. 5. Education & Institutional Partnership – 10% Develop and deliver training and guidance related to employee relations, civil rights, workplace conduct, investigations, accommodations, reporting responsibilities, and related compliance matters. Build effective partnerships across Human Resources, Academic Affairs, Student Affairs, Legal Affairs, Public Safety, and other University functions. Participate in institutional committees, working groups, and initiatives related to employee relations, civil rights, compliance, and workplace effectiveness. Support policy review, process improvement, and prevention initiatives. Perform other duties consistent with the scope and level of the position. Requisite Experience Seven to ten years of progressively responsible experience in employee relations, workplace investigations, civil rights compliance, Title IX, Human Resources, employment compliance, or a related field. Demonstrated experience independently managing complex civil rights investigations and sensitive employee relations investigations. Experience advising leaders on performance management, corrective action, workplace conflict, policy interpretation, and employee concerns. Bachelor’s degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Legal Studies, Organizational Leadership, Higher Education Administration, Psychology, or a related field required. Functional & Professional Competencies Advanced employee relations consultation and problem-solving. Workplace and civil rights investigation expertise. Working knowledge of Title VI, Title VII, Title IX, ADA, FMLA, and related employment and civil rights requirements. Strong case management and documentation capabilities. Ability to assess complex situations, identify risks, and develop practical recommendations. Strong consultation, facilitation, conflict-resolution, and relationship-management skills. Ability to influence leaders and stakeholders without formal authority. Strong written, verbal, analytical, and organizational skills. Demonstrated judgment, impartiality, discretion, and professionalism when handling sensitive matters. Ability to recognize matters requiring escalation based on legal, institutional, reputational, or operational risk"
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001218156206": {
      "title": "Assistant Baseball Coach",
      "description": "Assistant Baseball Coach. General Statement of Duties: The assistant coach provides instructional, administrative, and recruiting support services under the direction of the Head Coach and Director of Athletics. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* Assists the Head Coach in the implementation of sport training through teaching and instructing the student-athletes in the rules and fundamentals of baseball. Under the direction of the Head Coach, assists in providing sport skill instruction, assists in the conduct of practice sessions, performs game related duties, and motivates players to give their best efforts. Contributes to recruiting efforts through on-campus visits and off-campus evaluation. Supports team fundraising initiatives. Mentors student athletes. Demonstrates sincere interest in the personal, academic and athletic success of the student-athlete. Represents the University in a positive manner at all times. Abides by all SLIAC and NCAA rules and regulations. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Assisting other coaches in the department with game management duties. May supervise student assistants and/or student managers. Functional/Technical Skills* Demonstrated baseball coaching knowledge. Excellent interpersonal, teaching and communication skills. General knowledge of NCAA Division III rules. Bachelor’s Degree from an accredited college or university. Minimum one (1) year of experience in coaching baseball or other related baseball activities. Strong communications skills, both written and verbal, and interpersonal skills. Strong organizational skills. Ability to work on multiple projects simultaneously. Ability to exercise important human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the university community and the public. Ability to maintain composure when faced with difficult situations and to effectively identify and resolve problems and to maintain strict confidentiality related to sensitive information. Demonstrated commitment to diversity and experience promoting inclusive excellence."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001187575106": {
      "title": "Instructional Media Specialist",
      "description": "Instructional Media Specialist. Description of Work The Instructional Media Specialist is responsible for the ownership, operation, and continuous improvement of the university’s academic video production and instructional media ecosystem , ensuring that video functions as a reliable, accessible, and pedagogically sound core teaching medium across Webster University’s online and continuing education digital learning initiatives. This role combines academic media platform stewardship , high-quality instructional media production , faculty development and support , and accessibility accountability . The position is embedded in the course and continuing education development and revision lifecycle and serves as a required dependency for credit-bearing instruction, faculty training and support, and institutional digital learning initiatives . The Instructional Media Specialist plans, directs, shoots, edits, and produces professional instructional media, including documentary-style field productions, instructional demonstrations, interview recordings, faculty-led instructional video with integrated teaching graphics, and motion-graphics - based instructional media . The role serves as the primary steward of the university’s academic video platform (Kaltura) , overseeing media organization, delivery, integration with Canvas, and distribution within course and continuing education learning environments. The position ensures all instructional video meets accessibility standards and supports faculty in the effective design, creation, and delivery of instructional media. The role requires a blend of creative production expertise and technical leadership , inc luding motion graphics, audio production, instructional video design, platform management, faculty consultation, and accessibility best practices. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Serve as the primary owner and steward of the university’s academic video platform (Kaltura) , in cluding configuration, organization, permissions, integration with Canvas, and media distribution within course environments. Ensure the reliable, accessible, and pedagogically appropriate use of instructional video across online and continuing education courses, faculty training, and institutional digital learning initiatives. Plan, direct, shoot, edit, and publish high-quality instructional media in sup port of online and continuing education instruction and academic programs, including documentary-style field productions, instructional demonstrations, interviews, faculty-led instructional videos, and motion-graphics–based instructional media. Collaborate with faculty, instructional designers, and academic units to design, produce, and integrate instructional media aligned with course outcomes, teaching strategies, and accessibility standards. Provid e faculty development and consultation rel ated to instructional video design, production practices, and effective use of media within Canvas courses. Ensure instructional media accessibility compliance, including oversight of captions, transcripts, audio descriptions, and remediation workflows for instructional video content, in alignment with academic accessibility standards and regulatory requirements . Produce and mix high-quality audio for instructional media, including voiceovers and live recordings, ensuring clarity and instructional effectiveness. Create instructional motion graphics and visual teaching assets, independently or in collaboration with designers. Monitor platform updates, emerging tools, and instructional media practices, and make institutional recommendations to improve academic media workflows, standards, and system use. Troubleshoot instructional video playback, embedding, and integration issues within learning management systems and academic platforms. Participate in onlin e and continuing education course development, redesign, content development , and cross-functional design sprints as a required media production and platform dependency . Contribute to the continuous improvement of instructional media standards, workflows, and visual communication practices in support of online learning. Perform other duties as assigned in support of academic instruction, faculty development, and digital learning initiatives . This is a part-time position located at our Webster Groves, Missouri campus. Qualifications For Appointment Ability to own and operate an academic instructional media ecosystem , including video production workflows, platform use, and media delivery within learning environments. Demonstrated proficiency in instructional video production , including camera operation, lighting, audio capture, editing, and post-production. Strong skills in instructional motion graphics and visual explanation , including the use of animated text, graphics, and teaching visuals to support learning outcomes. Working knowledge of video accessibility principles , including captions, transcripts, and audio descriptions, and the ability to apply accessibility best practices to instructional media. Ability to manage multiple concurrent projects , balancing long-term course development timelines with time-sensitive instructional and training needs. Strong interpersonal and communication skills , including the ability to collaborate with faculty, instructional designers, and non-technical stakeholders in both in-person and virtual environments. Self-directed with strong problem-solving skills , sound judgment, and a high degree of personal accountability. Collaborative mindset with the ability to contribute effectively to cross-functional academic and digital learning teams . Demonstrated commitment to continuous learning and adapting to evolving instructional media tools, platforms, and technologies. Please include a link to a professional reel or representative instructional media work as part of the application. Requisite Experience ​ ​ Bachelor’s degree in film production, video production, media arts, or related field, or equivalent professional experienc e . ​ Minimum of two years of professional instructional or media production experience . ​ Experience planning, directing, and shooting field-based and studio-based video productions using DSLR or comparable camera systems. ​ Experience with location and studio audio recording , including boom and lavalier microphone techniques. ​ Professional experience using Adobe Creative Suite , including Premiere Pro and After Effects; experience with Illustrator and Photoshop for instructional media assets. ​ Experience producing motion graphics and post-production visual elements , including green screen workflows. ​ Experience preparing, transcoding, publishing, and archiving video for web-based and platform-based delivery . ​ Familiarity with basic web publishing concepts , including HTML and CSS, as they relate to media embedding and delivery. ​ Experience working with a range of standard software tools, including productivity applications, browsers, and media platforms."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001221163406": {
      "title": "Assistant Volleyball Coach - Seasonal",
      "description": "Assistant Volleyball Coach - Seasonal. Primary duty is to assist the head coach in the implementation of sport training through teaching and instructing the student-athletes in the rules and fundamentals of volleyball. Under the direction of the head coach, will assist in providing sport skill instruction, assist in the conduct of practice sessions, perform game related duties, and motivate players to give their best efforts. Contribute to recruiting efforts through on-campus visits and off-campus evaluation. Support team fundraising initiatives. Mentor student athletes. Demonstrate sincere interest in the personal, academic and athletic success of the student-athlete. Represent the University in a positive manner at all times. Abide by all SLIAC and NCAA rules and regulations. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* Assisting other coaches in the department with game management duties. May supervise student assistants and/or student managers. Functional/Technical Skills* Demonstrated volleyball coaching knowledge. Excellent interpersonal, teaching and communication skills. General knowledge of NCAA Division III rules. Bachelor’s Degree from an accredited college or university. Minimum one (1) year of experience in coaching volleyball or other related volleyball activities. Strong communications skills, both written and verbal, and interpersonal skills. Strong organizational skills. Ability to work on multiple projects simultaneously. Ability to exercise important human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the university community and the public. Ability to maintain composure when faced with difficult situations and to effectively identify and resolve problems and to maintain strict confidentiality related to sensitive information. Demonstrated commitment to diversity and experience promoting inclusive excellence."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001219944406": {
      "title": "Athletics Coordinator",
      "description": "Athletics Coordinator. General Statement of Duties : Plans and executes various activities in support of the Athletics Department. Assists with department finances and budgets, special events, student employment, Human Resources, and day-to-day administrative duties. Serves as the Athletic Department liaison to the Webster University community and outside entities. Exercises discretion and independent judgment in the performance of duties. Reports to the Director of Athletics. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* Performs administrative clerical and office support duties in support of the Director of Athletics and the head coaches. Advises department on university policies and procedures. Assists with budgetary strategies for department entities; monitoring and tracking budget for department and teams, monitoring team specific fundraising initiatives, and reconciling P-Card monthly reports for department staff while ensuring compliance with university policies. Processes all check requisitions and deposits for Athletic Department. Uses financial system to complete and submit annual surveys with U.S. Department of Education and NCAA Gender Equity reports. Provides institutional, departmental, conference and NCAA information in person or by telephone to prospective or current students, parents of current or prospective students, college administrators, alumni, and the public at large. Assists Compliance Officer with NCAA Compliance for the Athletic Department. Assists with maintaining and updating confidential student Compliance records regarding student-athlete eligibility for approximately 280 student-athletes. Assists with records maintenance, organizes and evaluates NCAA paperwork to ensure compliance with University and NCAA policies. Works with head coaches as issues arise with their student-athletes and/or teams and keeps staff up to date with any rule changes. Works with WU Human Resources and Finances to coordinate department’s new hires, promotions, and separation paperwork. Assist with new hire onboarding and address any inquiries about positions during the transition phase. Assists Student Employment Supervisor with student employment positions. Works with Career Services to process paperwork for 85-90 student employees. Assists with paperwork submission, verifying student work study eligibility, and tracking students’ earnings. Responsible for hiring, training and supervising office assistant student workers. Coordinates implementation for department special events including but not limited to the Gorlok Open, senior nights, parents’ receptions, Athletic Awards Ceremony, hosting conference and regional tournaments, reserving rooms for NCAA tournament watch parties, and Hall of Fame Induction. Assists with the negotiation and approval of contacts for team ground travel (buses and rental vans). Confirms game schedules, assist with game day management and operations, including locker room signage, coordinating the reservation of officials parking with Public Safety and reserving rooms in the University Center. Maintains favorable liaison with college campus personnel both internally and externally. Recommends improvements in organization and takes lead on action. Stays abreast of university programs, policies, and procedures. Responds to public inquiries in a courteous manner; receives and resolves student problems and complaints; refers serious problems and complaints to immediate supervisor. Provide operating instruction on various office equipment such as computers, fax, copier, telephone, etc. Performs other duties as assigned. Bachelor's Degree from an accredited college or university. Strong organizational skills, detail-oriented. Ability to work on multiple projects simultaneously. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to exercise important human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the university community and the public. Ability to maintain composure when faced with difficult situations and to effectively identify and resolve problems and to maintain strict confidentiality related to sensitive information. Demonstrated commitment to diversity and experience promoting inclusive excellence."
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001218417606": {
      "title": "Centre Francophone (CF) Fellow",
      "description": "Centre Francophone (CF) Fellow. The CF Fellow serves to promote the Centre Francophone (CF) and French-related events at Webster. This part-time position will be responsible for building and strengthening community relations between the university and French/Francophone related organizations, educational institutions (e.g., high schools, community colleges), and corporate and other partners to attract and generate interest in French study at Webster. Essential Duties and Responsibilities* · Facilitate the Seal of Biliteracy recognition by the university · Develop internal and external partnerships with French-related organizations (i.e., AATF, Alliance Française de St. Louis, et al.), educational institutions, and corporate and other partners to strengthen academic-community partnerships · Organize French/Francophone related activities and events at Webster to promote French language and culture to existing and prospective students in collaboration with relevant faculty · Conduct outreach to local area educational institutions (high schools, community colleges, et al.) to promote French study at Webster · Promote study abroad in French-speaking countries to current students in collaboration with Study Abroad as appropriate · Maintain CF website, social media, and other communication platforms as needed Secondary Duties and Responsibilities* · Identify opportunities for internships and other experiential learning opportunities for students as appropriate Functional/Technical Skills* · Native or fluent French speaker with deep knowledge and understanding of French/Francophone cultures · Demonstrated ability to develop partnerships across diverse stakeholders and effectively interact with people from different social and cultural backgrounds · Experience organizing educational, cultural, or community events and working collaboratively with student populations · Excellent communication skills (written and oral) with timely and professional responsiveness to emails and requests · Strong organizational skills and multi-tasker with the ability to handle multiple priorities and consistently meet deadlines · Proficiency in Microsoft 365 and ability to learn new software as needed · Experience living, studying or traveling in France or French-related countries highly desirable"
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001217229606": {
      "title": "Sound Design Mentor",
      "description": "Sound Design Mentor. Webster University’s Sargent Conservatory of Theatre Arts invites applications for an adjunct faculty member — Sound Design Mentor ( The Crucible ) Essential Duties: Mentoring Sound Design students for Mainstage production in the Fall Meeting with the Sound Design students and attending tech rehearsals for The Crucible Bachelor's degree and/or extensive experience in the field"
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001209610106": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor of Living Anatomy and Movement",
      "description": "Adjunct Professor of Living Anatomy and Movement. Webster University in St. Louis is currently seeking an adjunct instructor for the Dance Program to teach Living Anatomy and Movement, an undergraduate course designed for dance majors. This course explores functional anatomy through movement, helping students develop an understanding of the body's structure and mechanics as they relate to dance technique, performance, conditioning, and injury prevention. Master's degree in Dance, Kinesiology, Exercise Science, Somatics, Physical Therapy, Athletic Training, Anatomy, or a closely related field. (A bachelor's degree with significant professional experience may be considered.)"
    },
    "https://myjobs.adp.com/websteruniversity/cx/job-details?reqId=5001208854806": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor Communications and Journalism",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor Communications and Journalism. Webster University in St. Louis is currently seeking adjunct instructors for the Communications and Journalism Department to teach courses in Journalism, Social Media, Media Analytics, Media Studies, and Strategic Communications which includes Advertising and Public Relations. Applicants must possess a MA degree and a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant field. Responsibilities include teaching one or more courses per semester either in classroom or online. Applications will be reviewed on an as-needed basis. Applicants must possess a MA degree and a minimum of five years of professional experience in the relevant field."
    },
